    "id": 152,
    "title": "Total solar eclipse 2026 has begun. Here are the first views of totality",
    "summary": "Comprehensive scientific analysis and dispatch reporting on Total solar eclipse 2026 has begun. Here are the first views of totality.",
    "content": "Eclipses Solar Eclipses Total solar eclipse 2026 has begun. Here are the first views of totality News By Anthony Wood Published 12 August 2026 Check out the first glorious views of totality for the total solar eclipse 2026, as the moon's shadow continues to race across the Northern Hemisphere.\n\nEclipse chasers have spent the past few hours watching in awe, as the moon transformed the sun into an incandescent crescent in the run up to totality, before swallowing it entirely in the skies over Scoresby Sund, Greenland as seen from the deck of the MS Spitsbergen on the HX Expeditions eclipse voyage.\n\nRead on to see spectacular photos of the sun's corona glowing in the false twilight of totality. And be sure to stay with us in the coming hours as the moon's central shadow sweeps over Europe, before ultimately disappearing from view off the north coast of Africa at 2:34 p.m. EDT (1834 GMT).\n\nThe first photos of totality revealed the sun's corona, visible thanks to the moon blocking most of our star's light.\n\nLuckily, weather cooperated for the eclipse chasers on the MS Spitsbergen on the HX Expeditions eclipse voyage in Scoresby Sund, Greenland.\n\nSpace.com's own skywatching editor Daisy Dobrijevic was onboard, capturing the first images of totality.\n\nAs the moon drifted past the sun, the first glimpses of the sun began to reveal themselves as the path of totality moved onwards towards Spain.\n\nWe'll have more coverage on our total solar eclipse live blog throughout the day, including reports from Skywatching Editor Daisy Dobrijevic as she catches the entirety of the eclipse unfolding in the skies over Greenland aboard an HX Expeditions cruise ship.\n\nSkywatching Writer Anthony Wood will also be giving you the live updates from his vantage point in central Spain, where the eclipsed sun will set below the horizon just a few short hours from now.\n\nEditor's Note: If you would like to share your eclipse photos with Space.com's readers, then please send your image(s), comments, name and location to spacephotos@space.com.",
    "url": "https://www.space.com/stargazing/solar-eclipses/total-solar-eclipse-has-begun-here-are-the-first-views-of-totality-aug-12-2026",
