    "id": 8,
    "title": "Total solar eclipse 2026: As it happened from Iceland",
    "summary": "Today, the Moon’s shadow will fall across the North Atlantic, sending Greenland, Iceland, and Spain into darkness for a total solar eclipse. This will be the first Iceland has seen since 1954, and the first over Reykjavík in almost six hundred years. Totality will reach Iceland in the afternoon local time and Spain in the evening, just before sunset. Astronomy’s Senior Editor Alison Klesman is on the ground in Iceland and Editor Emeritus and former Editor-in-Chief Dave Eicher is in Spain with our travel partner, Eclipse Traveler. They’ll be filing live updates as the eclipse unfolds.",
    "content": "Today, the Moon’s shadow will fall across the North Atlantic, sending Greenland, Iceland, and Spain into darkness for a total solar eclipse. This will be the first Iceland has seen since 1954, and the first over Reykjavík in almost six hundred years. Totality will reach Iceland in the afternoon local time and Spain in the evening, just before sunset. Astronomy’s Senior Editor Alison Klesman is on the ground in Iceland and Editor Emeritus and former Editor-in-Chief Dave Eicher is in Spain with our travel partner, Eclipse Traveler. They’ll be filing live updates as the eclipse unfolds.\n\n4:20 p.m. EDT: A birthday gift the size of the Sun\n\nNahel Belgherze (@WxNB_) posted this incredible footage of totality as seen from Burgos, Spain, on X.com around 2:53 p.m. EDT. It was truly a once-in-a-lifetime experience for Belgherze, as today’s eclipse aligned with their birthday.\n\nTOTALITY.Castrojeriz, Burgos, Spain minutes ago. pic.twitter.com/KOZBmwQKuw\n\n2:35 p.m. EDT: The ISS transits the Sun during the 2026 solar eclipse\n\nNASA shared this image around 1:44 p.m. EDT of the International Space Station as it passed in front of the Sun, as seen from northeast Maine near the Canadian border.\n\nThe @Space_Station, with a crew of seven onboard, is seen as it transits the Sun at approximately five miles per second during a partial solar eclipse as seen near Hodgdon, Maine. 📷: https://t.co/7o5Z3IlCyi pic.twitter.com/99WtPdZ5x2\n\n2:31 p.m. EDT: Totality ends in Spain\n\n2:29 p.m. EDT: Totality begins in Spain\n\nClear, sunny skies provide better viewing conditions during totality in Spain.\n\nWe're lucky enough to experience the eclipse again, as we reach totality in León, Spain. pic.twitter.com/jzH66vYdkA\n\n2:21 p.m. EDT: Cloudy with a chance of totality in Iceland",
    "url": "https://www.astronomy.com/observing/total-solar-eclipse-2026-live-updates-from-iceland-and-spain/",
