    "id": 76,
    "title": "Investigation of Mode Changing and Subpulse Drifting Properties of Three New Pulsars Discovered by FAST",
    "abstract": "Abstract\n                  \n                    Studying pulsars with interesting single-pulse emission features in depth is one of the main ways to understand how pulsar radiation works. In this paper, we present a detailed investigation of three newly discovered pulsars that show unusual single-pulse behavior, namely PSRs J1745−0059, J1800−0059, and J1801−0108. Using the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope, we reveal their unique single-pulse radiation properties. Our results show that J1745−0059 is a pulsar that exhibits both mode changing and nulling. We find that nulling events are fairly brief, and the overall nulling fraction is about 2.1% ± 0.3%. The pulsar also shows two distinct emission modes: 67% of the time it is in Mode A, and 33% of the time in Mode B. The key difference between the two modes is the strength of the trailing component, which is greater in Mode A. Moreover, during Mode A, the trailing component displays intensity modulation with a period of about 4.2\n                    P\n                     ± 0.4\n                    P\n                    , where\n                    P\n                    is the pulsar’s spin period. For J1800−0059 and J1801−0108, we detect clear subpulse drifting, with modulation periods of\n                    P\n                    3\n                     = 5.0\n                    P\n                     ± 0.4\n                    P\n                    and\n                    P\n                    3\n                     = 19.9\n                    P\n                     ± 2.3\n                    P\n                    , respectively. Furthermore, J1801−0108 may exhibit a reversed drift direction. Lastly, we obtain the polarization and geometric properties for all three pulsars.",
